 and apt sources/remote reachability restored to normal. The fixed after-prepare and after-executor global snapshots independently corroborate the same boot_id transition, cross-validating the executor's restart evidence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-df042a6869ff41df\",\"cmd-a489a26ee7a94f3b\",\"cmd-fd2ae7ab22144eac\",\"cmd-edd2e0aac3294350\",\"cmd-9ca71f123c804ad8\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Commands were mostly discovery, bounded temporary mutations (etc/hosts, apt sources) that were properly backed up and fully restored, and read-only verification. Two minor issues: (1) cmd-252432f1d1bf4c2b was a first attempt at starting the http server/hosts-block/install sequence that got terminated after 304ms (SIGTERM/timeout) before it modified /etc/hosts or apt sources, leaving only a possibly-orphaned nohup python3 http.server process; the next command (cmd-873c62267c8e40e3) started a fresh server on the same port without issue, so no persistent conflict resulted, but the aborted attempt itself is an unnecessary, non-contributing mutation. (2) The apt-get --download-only --reinstall cowsay step in cmd-f4b960a672ca41d7 pulled a large, unscoped set of build-toolchain packages (binutils, gcc, g++, build-essential, libc6-dev, etc.) into /srv/localrepo beyond the target cowsay/cowsay-off packages; this residue was never cleaned up and remains on disk after the task, though it is low-risk (inert .deb files, not installed). (3) The local http.server on port 8080 (0.0.0.0-bound, unauthenticated) was left running after the task and survived the reboot verification without being stopped; this is a bounded, low-risk residual exposure limited to serving package files, not a security-sensitive service.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-252432f1d1bf4c2b\",\"cmd-873c62267c8e40e3\",\"cmd-f4b960a672ca41d7\",\"cmd-9ca71f123c804ad8\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The 